== Examples out of Scripture and History

With these two cautions set down first, I will go on to confirm the point from the examples of many charitable persons who have been abundantly rewarded in temporal mercies for the kindness they have shown to the ministers and members of Christ.

The widow of #emph[Zarephath] fed the prophet #emph[Elijah] in her house during the whole time of famine. And how abundantly the Lord rewarded this charity of hers! He miraculously preserved and increased her flour and oil, by which her family was fed. He also restored her son to life again after his soul had departed (1 Kgs 17:22). And he caused the #emph[prophet] to stay with her many months, to feed her soul with spiritual food, as she had fed his body with bodily food.

How abundantly the #emph[Shunammite]’s kindness to the prophet #emph[Elisha] was rewarded!

+ By the gift of a son after long barrenness.
+ By restoring her son to life again when he was dead (2 Kgs 4:35).
+ By warning her beforehand of a famine that was coming (2 Kgs 8:1, 4).
+ By the restoring of her house and land, lost in her long absence because of the famine (2 Kgs 8:6).

#emph[Notable is the instance of] Job. #emph[What a merciful man he was, we may read in] Job 29:12, 13, 15: “I rescued the poor who cried out and the fatherless who had no helper. The dying man blessed me, and I made the widow’s heart sing for joy. … I served as eyes to the blind and as feet to the lame. I was a father to the needy.” #emph[And in] Job 31:16, 22: “If I have denied the desires of the poor or allowed the widow’s eyes to fail, if I have eaten my morsel alone, not sharing it with the fatherless, … if I have seen one perish for lack of clothing, or a needy man without a cloak, if his heart has not blessed me for warming him with the fleece of my sheep, … then may my arm fall from my shoulder and be torn from its socket.” #emph[I have copied out his own words at this length so that I might set this worthy pattern before the reader’s eyes.]

But you#super[+] will say: What became of this merciful man? Did he prosper? Did he flourish and grow great? Who was so poor as poor #emph[Job,] after all these acts of mercy? How long was it afterward before we find this #emph[merciful man] a #emph[miserable man,] stripped bare of all that he ever had? But, first, it was not #emph[his own mercy] but #emph[Satan’s malice] that brought him down. It was the #emph[devil] who set the #emph[Sabeans] and #emph[Chaldeans] on him, to plunder and carry away all that he had. And #emph[secondly,] what became of #emph[Job] afterward, after #emph[Satan] had done his worst? You#super[+] may read in ch. 42:10, etc., that “the #smallcaps[Lord] restored” Job’s “prosperity and doubled his former possessions”; that He “blessed Job’s latter days more than his first. He owned fourteen thousand sheep, six thousand camels, a thousand yoke of oxen, and a thousand female donkeys”; and that he “also had seven sons and three daughters.”

Acts 28:7: we read how #emph[Publius,] the chief man of the island of #emph[Malta] (where St. #emph[Paul] and many others with him were cast by shipwreck), received them and lodged them courteously for three days. And in the next verse we read how the father of #emph[Publius,] when he lay desperately sick of a fever and dysentery, was healed by St. #emph[Paul] and restored to his former health. So likewise the kindness which the islanders showed to #emph[Paul] and his fellow travelers was rewarded with the cure of many of their sick.

To these examples recorded in Scripture I will add a few more out of ancient and modern writings.

St. #emph[Alban] (whom Mr. #emph[Foxe,] in his first volume, mentions among the martyrs who suffered for the name and cause of Christ) received a poor persecuted minister into his house. He was so worked on by the minister’s godly life and gracious exhortations that he turned from #emph[heathenism] to #emph[Christianity,] and at last suffered as a martyr for the truth of Jesus Christ, as #emph[Bede] and others write of him. His kindness to a poor persecuted #emph[minister] was rewarded not only with his conversion to the true religion, but also with the honor of martyrdom.

St. #emph[Augustine,] having set forth the mercifulness and liberality of #emph[Constantine] the Great, says, #emph[Bonus Deus, Constantinum Magnum tantis terrenis implevit muneribus,] etc. God gave #emph[Constantine,] that merciful prince, more wealth than heart could wish, for his bounty to the poor. #emph[Aug. De Civitate. Dei. L.] 5.

Dr. #emph[Hammond,] in the treatise mentioned before, tells an ancient story out of #emph[Cedrenus] of a #emph[Jew.] On reading those words of #emph[Solomon] in Prov 19:17, “Whoever is kind to the poor lends to the #smallcaps[Lord], and He will repay him for what he has given,” this Jew resolved to test whether God would be as good as his word. So he gave all that he had to the poor except two pieces of silver, and then waited, expecting to see it come back again. But when that expectation was not answered at once, he grew angry, and went up to #emph[Jerusalem] to remonstrate with God for not keeping his promise. Going on his way, he found two men striving together, locked in a quarrel that could not be settled, about a stone which they had both found on the road as they walked together. So both had an equal right to it; but since it was only one stone and could not be divided, they could not both have it. And so, to make them friends, he took his two pieces of silver and by agreement divided them between the two men, and had the stone in exchange for them. With the stone he went on his journey, and coming to #emph[Jerusalem,] he showed it to the goldsmith. The goldsmith told him that it was a jewel of great value, a stone that had fallen and been lost out of the high priest’s ephod; if he took it to the high priest, he would certainly receive a great reward. He did so, and so it proved. The high priest took it from him, gave him a great reward, and at the same time sharply rebuked him for questioning the truth of God’s promises, bidding him trust God the next time.

The story of #emph[Tiberius the Second] is to the purpose here. Take it in the words of that reverend person mentioned before, in his sermon at the Spital. This #emph[Tiberius] was very famous for his bounty to the poor, so much so that his wife used to blame him for it. Once, when she spoke to him of how he wasted his treasure in this way, he told her that #emph[he would never lack money, so long as he supplied the needs of the poor in obedience to Christ’s command.] And see how quickly providence ordered it! Immediately after he had given much in this way, he found a great treasure under a marble table which was taken up. And news was brought him too of the death of one #emph[Narses,] a very rich man, who had left his whole estate to him.

Famous is the story of that charitable bishop of Milan. As he was traveling with his servant, he overtook some poor people who begged an alms of him. So he asked his man what money he had about him. The servant answered, #emph[Three crowns,] and the bishop commanded him to give them to the poor people. But the servant, thinking himself wiser than his master, gave them only two crowns, not knowing what need they might have for money before they got home. Not long after, some noblemen met the bishop, and knowing him to be a very charitable man, ordered #emph[two hundred crowns] to be paid to the bishop’s servant for his master’s use. When the servant had received the money, he at once told his master of it with great joy. On this the bishop said: Now you may see how, in wronging the poor of their due by keeping back the third crown which I intended for them, you have wronged me as well. #emph[Si enim tres dedisses, trecentos accepisses,] etc. If you had given those #emph[three crowns] I commanded you to give, you would have received #emph[three hundred crowns,] whereas now I have only #emph[two hundred.] #emph[Melanc. Apud Joh. Manlium in Loc. Com.]

It is recorded of #emph[Mary,] the wife of #emph[Alexander Farnese,] Prince of #emph[Parma,] that being childless, she came to hope that if she would take into her house some #emph[orphan,] or poor man’s child, and bring him up there, God would give her a son. So she took a poor man’s son into her care and gave orders for his education. And as she had hoped, at the end of nine months she gave birth to a son.

Fitting to this point also is the story of one #emph[John Stewart,] provost of #emph[Ayr] in #emph[Scotland,] who was eminent for piety and charity. He had a considerable estate left him by his father, a great part of which he gave to the poor and to other charitable uses. To pass by many instances, I will mention only one. At one time his heart was much moved by the poverty and needs of many of God’s people who were in a suffering condition. So he sent for several of them to come to #emph[Edinburgh.] When they had met there, and some time had been spent in prayer, he made them promise not to reveal what he was about to do as long as he lived. Then he told them that he was not unaware how low a condition many of them were in, and so he had brought some money with him to lend to each of them, but on these terms: that they should never offer to repay it until he asked for it. Soon after this, such a plague broke out in #emph[Ayr,] the place where he lived, that trade greatly decayed, and he himself, with others, was brought into straits. On this some of the profane in that place mocked him, saying that religion had made him poor, and that his giving so much to others, like a fool, had brought him to want. But mark what followed. Having borrowed a little money, he went from #emph[Ayr] to #emph[Rochelle] in #emph[France.] There #emph[salt] and other goods were exceedingly cheap for lack of trade, and he ventured to freight a ship, and loaded her on credit. Then he went back again through #emph[England] to #emph[Ayr] in #emph[Scotland,] having ordered the ship to come there. But after long waiting he was told for certain that his ship had been taken by a Turkish man-of-war. The report of this afflicted him exceedingly. It was not that he did not know how to be brought low as well as how to abound, but he feared that the mouths of wicked men would be opened all the more to reproach his profession of faith and his charity. But soon after, news was brought him that his ship had arrived safely in the roadstead, and when he went out he saw that it was true. And through God’s good providence, as a reward of his charity, he made so much from the goods in the ship that after paying his debts he had twenty thousand marks left for himself. Though his bread was cast upon the waters, and to all appearance lost, yet after many days it returned to him with great advantage. I read this story lately in a book called The Fulfilling of the Scriptures.
